   "text": "The drawing shows a 4-substituted benzoic acid joined through a two-carbon chain to the nitrogen of a pyrrolidin-2-one. The lactam ring carbon next to nitrogen carries a 3-hydroxynonyl chain.\n\n**Skeleton**\n- The ring is a five-membered lactam: N, C=O, two CH2 groups, and a CH bearing the side chain.\n- The N-substituent is CH2CH2 linked to a para-substituted benzene ring carrying CO2H.\n- The side chain on the ring CH has vertices at CH2, CH2, CH(OH), and then five more carbons ending in the terminal CH3. That makes a 3-hydroxynonyl chain, with the OH on C3.\n\n**Stereochemistry**\n- Lactam ring carbon (wedge to the chain): the CIP priorities are N, then ring CH2, then the chain CH2, then H. The ring CH2 outranks the chain CH2 because its path reaches the C=O carbon (O,O,N) sooner. With H pointing back, N \u2192 ring CH2 \u2192 chain runs counterclockwise, so this centre is S. In the SMILES this is `[C@H]`.\n- Carbinol carbon (hashed OH): the priorities are OH, then the chain branch toward the ring, then the chain branch toward the terminal CH3, then H. With OH hashed and H toward the viewer, the sense of the OH \u2192 ring-side \u2192 CH3-side sequence is reversed, so this centre is also S. In the SMILES this is `[C@@H]`.\n\nSMILES: O=C1CC[C@H](CC[C@@H](O)CCCCCC)N1CCc1ccc(C(=O)O)cc1"
